# Home Assistant controls and flows

Use these specifications when designing or reviewing a household task in Home Assistant. They combine the civica.uk [brand rules](../styles/brand-colour.html), [writing guide](../content/index.html) and reusable components with the capabilities that each integration actually provides.

The catalogue covers the original 30 design gaps recorded by the [component audit](../community/missing-components.html), with additional families identified by the [item-by-item interface census](interface-coverage.html). Use that census to find a specific built-in card, selector, control, settings route or domain. A specification explains required behaviour and how to check it. Its invented examples do not connect to a household, replace a native editor or prove that an installed integration supports a command.

The [terminology supplement](../content/home-assistant-terminology.html) explains native names and literal keys. The [UK systems review](../community/uk-design-system-review.html) records the applicable public-service pattern or the reason for a local extension.

## Start with state and actions

Read [entity state, confidence and freshness](entity-state.html) before formatting a reading. Read [action results](action-results.html) before adding a command. These contracts distinguish a request, its acceptance and the state subsequently reported by a device. They also distinguish an unavailable entity, an unknown value, a valid zero and an old retained value.

